        year = 2026,
        summary = "Income support as of right when work is lost, paid from contributions, with a tax-funded floor for people without them. After a set period, claimants must take up offered retraining or work; short-time work cuts hours before jobs; and the floor is paid without a job-search test, so the two routes can be compared.",
        details = "Keeps: income security with mandatory participation for claimants, from Nordic social democracy and Denmark's flexicurity model, whose effectiveness point is unconfirmed; short-time work, from Keynesian welfare capitalism and Germany's Kurzarbeit short-time work scheme, whose 'no sign of a causal effect' is not proof of no effect; a floor paid without a job-search test, from Universal basic income and Finland's basic income trial for unemployed people, whose results are preliminary. Blocks: easy dismissal with thin income support, from Monetarist neoliberalism, safeguarded by earnings-related benefits and the floor; hidden unemployment, from Command socialism, safeguarded by published counts of jobless and underemployed people. Test: for two years after a job loss, compare job-finding and pay on re-employment between the two routes, using register data, and track hours against headcount in short-time schemes. Source: Kreiner and Svarer, Journal of Economic Perspectives (2022); Institute for Employment Research (IAB), discussion paper 17/2014; Social Insurance Institution of Finland (Kela), The Basic Income Experiment 2017-2018 in Finland: Preliminary Results (2019); Kornai, The Socialist System",
